const queries=`-- Short account change, inside BEGIN / COMMIT:
UPDATE accounts
SET revision = revision + 1
WHERE account_id = 1
RETURNING revision;

-- Longer spending report:
SELECT account_id, sum(total)::text AS spent
FROM orders
GROUP BY account_id
ORDER BY sum(total) DESC, account_id
LIMIT 10;`;
const activity=`SELECT pid, application_name, state,
       wait_event_type, wait_event,
       clock_timestamp() - xact_start AS transaction_age,
       pg_blocking_pids(pid) AS blockers
FROM pg_stat_activity
WHERE datname = current_database()
  AND pid <> pg_backend_pid();`;
const sample=`At about 41 ms in the four-slot before run:
worker-1  active               Lock    transactionid  blockers: [38113]
worker-3  idle in transaction  Client  ClientRead      pid: 38113

worker-1 cannot finish its UPDATE until worker-3 ends its transaction.`;
---
<p>A short account change takes about 14–15 ms alone. A spending report takes about 4.4–4.5 ms. Put them together at a higher arrival rate, and the account changes’ observed p95 rises above half a second. The report’s database work remains short, yet its callers wait almost as long.</p>
<p>The missing time belongs to the workload: requests waiting for admission, executions competing for resources, and transactions waiting for another transaction to finish. This article follows one mixed workload from that symptom to a measured remedy. First finish <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">Understanding and improving database performance</a>, including <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/#change-one-explanation-into-a-measured-improvement">its comparison method</a>. We apply that method here to concurrent work, rather than treating a fast isolated plan as the end of the investigation.</p>
<nav aria-label="In this article"><ol>{sections.map(([id,title])=><li><a href={`#${id}`}>{title}</a></li>)}</ol></nav>

<h2 id="locate-where-the-extra-time-appears">Locate where the extra time appears</h2>
<p>The shop’s workload contains four account changes for each report. The changes increment account 1’s revision; the report ranks spending across 20,000 orders belonging to 100 accounts. Its values do not depend on that revision. The deliberately concentrated updates make one shared business record visible as a possible limit.</p>
<Sql code={queries} label="The two statement classes in the native workload" />
<p>The original request also performs independent preparation after the update, before committing. The worksheet represents that preparation with an imposed 12 ms application timer. It does no database work and needs no value protected by the transaction. This is a controlled explanation to test, not a claim that real applications spend exactly 12 ms there.</p>
<p>Measure from the request’s intended arrival to its completed result. Mark actual arrival, admission to an active slot, connection acquisition, first statement, transaction completion and response handling. These intervals locate different responsibilities. In this worksheet, connections are opened and warmed in advance; an active slot hands out one of them. The queue therefore measures waiting for a reusable connection, without connection-creation or HTTP overhead.</p>
<p>The interval around a driver call includes server work, server waits, network exchange and decoding. An observed execution plan answers a narrower question and normally does not send the query’s result to the client. PostgreSQL’s state <code>active</code> does not mean consuming CPU continuously. A statement can be active while waiting for a lock.</p>
<p>Figure 1 moves a request’s wait across the admission boundary. Play each version and compare when the caller gets its answer, rather than comparing only the connection interval.</p>
<LoadFigure scene="boundary" number={1} />
<p>Both model requests finish at 100 ms. The second version merely lets the request enter earlier and wait inside. Conversely, a shorter database interval can conceal a longer application queue. <a href="/review/relational-next/connections/">Managing connections and transaction lifetimes</a> owns pool implementation and deadlines; the performance comparison must retain both sides of that boundary.</p>
<p>Hold parameter classes steady when comparing alone and together. A common account and an unusually large account are different work. Here all changes target account 1, reports use the same interval-free aggregation, and the report plan scans 20,000 orders, aggregates 100 groups and ranks ten. Its isolated capture uses 109 shared-buffer hits and no temporary blocks. That evidence does not explain the later application queue.</p>

<h2 id="count-arrivals-completions-and-outstanding-work-together">Count arrivals, completions and outstanding work together</h2>
<p>Choose a boundary before naming a rate. Our outer boundary begins at intended arrival and ends when the driver has decoded success or failure. <strong>Offered demand</strong> counts attempted arrivals. <strong>Admission</strong> starts active connection ownership. A <strong>useful completion</strong> is a successful operation with its expected result. Count rejected arrivals, errors and remaining work separately.</p>
<p>Over an interval, ending outstanding work equals starting outstanding work plus accepted arrivals minus terminal completions and failures. Here accepted arrivals include requests still waiting for admission. A rejection never enters that population. State those choices explicitly: other systems use “admitted” for the outer acceptance decision instead.</p>
<p>A caller timing out does not necessarily end the database work. Keep a timed-out request outstanding until its underlying attempt finishes or cancellation and cleanup establish a terminal result. Count that terminal outcome once. A retry is another attempt and another source of demand; link its attempts to one business operation rather than calling two successful attempts two useful business completions. <a href="/review/relational-next/request-recovery/">Recovering database requests safely</a> owns that correctness and deadline protocol.</p>
<p>At 120 intended arrivals a second, the four-slot before run offers 240 operations over a two-second schedule. Only 188 finish by the two-second boundary; 52 remain. They finish during about 556 ms of subsequent drainage. Reporting 240 divided by the arrival interval would count work that had not finished yet. Reporting only the final empty queue would conceal the accumulation.</p>

<h3 id="an-average-relationship-needs-a-stable-population">An average relationship needs a stable population</h3>
<p>For a stable population observed over a representative interval, average outstanding work equals achieved throughput multiplied by average time inside the same boundary. This is Little’s law. In an illustrative service completing 100 operations a second with mean time 0.05 seconds, the average population is <code>100 × 0.05 = 5</code> operations. Include the admission queue in both the population and elapsed time, or exclude it from both. If the population includes failed work, use its total terminal departure rate for this relationship and report useful throughput separately.</p>
<p>The calculation needs consistent units, a stable long-run flow and a window that represents the population’s time in the system. It does not turn a p95 into a mean, predict the tail, or say to configure five connections. During growing overload, retain arrivals, departures and the queue’s changing size directly. During drainage, completions can exceed new arrivals because earlier work is leaving.</p>

<h3 id="a-waiting-client-can-hide-demand">A waiting client can hide demand</h3>
<p>A client that sends its next request only after receiving a reply is a <strong>closed-loop</strong> source. As replies slow, its offered rate falls. That can be realistic for a person waiting on one screen, but it cannot establish whether an independent stream of orders continues to arrive faster than the service finishes them.</p>
<p>Figure 2 gives the database one completion a second. Compare two independent arrivals each second with two clients that wait for their previous replies. Watch the arrivals as well as the outstanding work.</p>
<LoadFigure scene="arrivals" number={2} />
<p>The waiting clients reduce the input as the server slows. A test that records only their successful execution times misses requests an independent schedule would already have delivered. Our native worksheet schedules arrivals independently and retains intended and actual arrival timestamps. Its end-to-end latency starts at intended arrival, so scheduler delay and application waiting remain visible. If the generator itself falls behind, that lag is evidence to investigate, not server execution time to invent.</p>

<h2 id="find-what-the-concurrent-executions-are-competing-for">Find what the concurrent executions are competing for</h2>
<p>Collect evidence over the interval where the queue grows. Match request classes and connection identifiers to activity, plans and host observations. PostgreSQL activity samples describe moments; accumulated counters need before/after values, elapsed time and reset checks. Read statistics in fresh transactions when comparing intervals, because values can be cached within a transaction and updates can lag.</p>
<Sql code={activity} label="Find waiting sessions and the transactions ahead of them" />
<p>The worksheet samples its own workers about every 40 ms. In one before sample, a changing account waits for a transaction whose last statement has already finished:</p>
<pre class="a28-evidence"><code>{sample}</code></pre>
<p>Use <code>pg_blocking_pids</code> to follow the chain to its owner, then inspect that owner’s transaction and application trace. The function includes conflicting owners and waiters ahead in a lock queue. Calling it very frequently also has a cost. A sample can miss short waits; a blank wait event is not a CPU measurement.</p>

<h3 id="cpu-memory-and-storage-pressure">CPU, memory and storage pressure</h3>
<p>If many sessions are runnable, correlate host CPU utilisation and runnable competition with the affected classes. Ask whether repeated probes, unnecessary rows or expensive expressions increased their demand. If I/O waits and storage latency rise instead, inspect reads, writes and temporary work over that interval. An engine buffer read may hit the operating system’s cache; a high database cache-hit ratio neither proves cheap processing nor explains latency.</p>
<p>Plans can expose external sorts, hash batches and temporary-block traffic. Those are reasons to inspect intermediate work and memory use, not instructions to increase a global limit. <a href="/review/relational-next/query-plans/">Diagnosing a query from its execution plan</a> owns those reporting details. <a href="/review/relational-next/physical-storage/">How stored rows become physical work</a> explains locality, working sets and version retention.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL’s <code>work_mem</code> is a base allowance for an operation such as a sort, rather than one reservation per connection. Several operations and sessions may use it concurrently; hash operations also use <code>hash_mem_multiplier</code>. Figure 3 assumes two overlapping sorts in each process. Add reports and a parallel worker and watch the possible allowance multiply.</p>
<LoadFigure scene="memory" number={3} />
<p>The final 48 MiB is arithmetic for the stated assumptions, not observed resident memory or a complete server budget. Other memory remains, and real parallel implementations can differ in what they share. A scoped memory change needs evidence about concurrent operators and total pressure, as well as whether it removes a costly spill.</p>

<h3 id="a-hot-row-can-limit-a-mostly-idle-database">A hot row can limit a mostly idle database</h3>
<p>The account updates need conflicting access to one row. PostgreSQL normally retains their row locks until the transaction ends. Finishing the update statement does not release the row while its transaction waits for the application’s next command. More processors or faster scans cannot make those conflicting changes happen simultaneously.</p>
<p>Figure 4 follows two changes through the imposed preparation delay. Compare waiting after the update with preparing before acquiring a connection. Both versions increment the revision twice.</p>
<LoadFigure scene="hot" number={4} />
<p>The captured four-slot before run contains 130 sampled <code>Lock / transactionid</code> and 33 <code>Lock / tuple</code> observations. Some owners show <code>idle in transaction / ClientRead</code>. The owner is waiting for the application while another session waits for it. These are counts of sampled session states, not milliseconds or percentages of execution time.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL row-lock conflicts can appear as waits for another transaction’s identifier; not every row lock is listed as a tuple lock in <code>pg_locks</code>. Internal page and shared-structure synchronisation, reported in categories such as <code>LWLock</code>, is a different investigation. Start from the actual wait category and ownership evidence.</p>
<p>A serial dependency can coexist with spare host resources. We did not collect host CPU utilisation here, so the samples do not establish that this server was mostly idle. They do establish a blocking relationship and a transaction lifetime worth shortening. Changing isolation is no general remedy for two writers needing the same record. <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/">Making concurrent changes correct</a> owns lock guarantees, deadlocks and the invariant a redesign must preserve.</p>

<h3 id="background-work-and-old-transactions-change-the-competition">Background work and old transactions change the competition</h3>
<p>A slowdown that coincides with maintenance needs a shared timeline: foreground completions and waits beside vacuum progress, checkpoint activity and storage work. PostgreSQL’s checkpointer writes dirty pages; vacuum scans and cleans eligible versions. Either can compete with foreground work, while postponing necessary cleanup can enlarge later work. A checkpoint counter rising near an incident is not enough to make it the cause.</p>
<p>Also inspect old transaction and snapshot horizons. A long-lived relevant snapshot can retain versions that cleanup cannot yet remove, increasing the working set and future page processing. Transaction age alone does not establish which history is still needed. Use <a href="/review/relational-next/physical-storage/#updates-leave-work-behind-them">the physical explanation</a> to interpret the evidence, then <a href="/review/relational-next/maintenance-and-upgrades/">Maintaining and upgrading a relational service</a> for the maintenance procedure and its budget. These short native runs did not measure a maintenance cycle.</p>

<h2 id="match-the-intervention-to-the-limiting-resource">Match the intervention to the limiting resource</h2>
<p>Turn the evidence into a prediction. For this workload, shortening the account row’s ownership should remove lock queues without changing revision counts or report answers. Adding connections alone should eventually add waiters, because ownership remains serial. The comparison must still include reports waiting outside those connections.</p>

<h3 id="bound-the-work-in-progress">Bound the work in progress</h3>
<p>Test a few active limits instead of equating the server’s connection maximum with useful concurrency. Too few active slots can leave independent work waiting behind an occupied connection. Too many can increase CPU competition, memory demand or lock queues. The useful limit depends on the mix and on what each operation holds.</p>
<p>Figure 5 replays snapshots derived from the captured arrival and completion records. Change the active limit, then advance to 2,000 ms. Keep the application queue, active slots and useful completions in view together.</p>
<LoadFigure scene="comparison" number={5} />
<p>Before the change, one slot completes 165 operations by two seconds; two slots complete 189 and four complete 188. The small difference between two and four is not a meaningful ranking. Both leave more than 50 operations unfinished. One slot also makes reports wait behind the preparation of every earlier account update. Four slots cannot remove the account dependency.</p>
<p>Admission remains useful for bounding exposure even when it does not fix the bottleneck. Define what happens when its queue is full: reject, defer durably, or apply backpressure where the caller can honour it. Count those outcomes. Protect a report’s opportunity to start if short work has priority, and protect short requests from unlimited reports. Scheduling policies trade latency and fairness; a fast short class with starving long work is not whole-workload success. Pool and deadline mechanics remain with <a href="/review/relational-next/connections/">connection management</a>.</p>

<h3 id="reduce-work-and-shorten-serial-sections">Reduce work and shorten serial sections</h3>
<p>The tested remedy performs the same preparation before acquiring a connection, then begins, increments and commits. End-to-end time still includes the imposed 12 ms. The change removes that delay from both connection ownership and the row’s serial section. It preserves the atomic increment and does not weaken isolation or durability.</p>
<p>That move is legal only because the preparation is independent of protected database state. If it validates a balance that may change, the validation needs an appropriate check inside the transaction. If it sends an external effect, simply moving it can break atomicity or duplicate the effect. <a href="/review/relational-next/background-work/">Connecting transactions to background work and events</a> owns that design.</p>
<p>For another workload, remove repeated query work or give selective requests a better route before increasing resources. <a href="/review/relational-next/indexes/">Choosing indexes for a workload</a> owns key design and write cost. Batching can amortise round trips and commits, but larger batches also hold locks longer, use more memory and give other work fewer turns. Changing one hot summary into several independently updated records may remove a dependency only if the required invariant allows their separate ownership.</p>

<h3 id="reconsider-parallel-work-and-targeted-resources">Reconsider parallel work and targeted resources</h3>
<p>A parallel report can finish sooner alone while consuming workers, CPU, memory and storage bandwidth that short transactions need. Compare the report alongside those transactions with a few scoped parallelism choices. Accept the change from per-class latency and useful completions across the outer boundary, not from the report’s fastest isolated execution. This verification server has no parallel workers; Figure 3 explains a documented possibility, not an observed parallel comparison.</p>
<p>Add CPU when necessary runnable work is the supported limit; memory when a larger useful working set or reduced intermediate-file work predicts relief; storage capability when the relevant throughput or latency limit is demonstrated. Recheck the next bottleneck after the change. Provisioning, growth forecasts and normal or degraded headroom belong with <a href="/review/relational-next/monitoring-and-capacity/">Monitoring and sizing a relational service</a>, which consumes these workload measures.</p>

<h2 id="test-the-proposed-limit-without-hiding-the-overload">Test the proposed limit without hiding the overload</h2>
<p>The bounded sweep used 40 and 120 intended arrivals a second, one, two and four active slots, and the same four-update/one-report schedule. At each point it compared preparation inside the transaction with preparation before admission. Connections and statement paths were warmed first. Each schedule lasted two seconds, followed by complete queue drainage; the critical four-slot high-demand pair was repeated once.</p>
<p>The guard rejected new work at 80 outstanding operations, with 1.5-second statement and one-second lock timeouts and a six-second drainage stop. No run reached rejection or produced a database error. Every high-demand run eventually completed 192 updates and 48 reports. Final revision counts matched successful updates exactly, and every report matched the reference ordered answer. The worksheet records intended arrival, actual arrival, queue entry, connection acquisition, driver start, terminal result and activity samples.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Critical before and after workload comparison"><table><caption>Four slots · two runs per version · 240 arrivals per run · latency includes drainage</caption><thead><tr><th>Observation</th><th>Delay inside transaction</th><th>Prepare before admission</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Useful completions by 2 seconds</td><td>187–188</td><td>240</td></tr>
<tr><td>Outstanding at 2 seconds</td><td>52–53</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td>Update p95 from intended arrival</td><td>553.2–553.3 ms</td><td>15.6–15.9 ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>Report p95 from intended arrival</td><td>507.8–513.6 ms</td><td>7.7–7.8 ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>Additional drainage after 2 seconds</td><td>556–563 ms</td><td>0 ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>Errors / rejections</td><td>0 / 0</td><td>0 / 0</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The after samples contain no lock waits in either critical run. Together with shorter connection intervals, preserved answers and the disappearance of accumulation, this supports the proposed transaction-boundary change for the fixture. At the lower arrival rate, both versions already finish within the schedule and have similar end-to-end latency: moving the preparation does not make its 12 ms disappear. A single observation of <code>WalWrite</code>, or its absence, would not reverse that causal chain.</p>
<p>These are short observations on a shared native PostgreSQL 18.6 instance, with 32 MB shared buffers, 4 MB work memory and no parallel workers. Other article workers could use their own databases on the server. The run does not isolate server capacity, cover maintenance cycles or establish production p95. The regular hot-account pattern, small report and imposed timer deliberately expose one mechanism. No host CPU, device-I/O, WAL-rate or total-memory comparison was collected.</p>
<p>For deployment acceptance, use the parent’s method with the application’s actual skew, burst pattern, transaction lengths, common and rare parameters, result sizes and writes. Keep warm-up, sustained competition and drainage distinct. Retain p50, p95 and p99 by class with sample counts, failures, rejects and outstanding work. Check that a lower limit has not only moved a queue, that a faster report has not harmed short work, and that today’s gain has not deferred cleanup or increased write cost. A bounded observed range is evidence for a decision under those conditions, not a universal capacity promise.</p>
<p>To repeat the native evidence, <a href={worksheet} download="concurrent-load-verify.mjs">download the worksheet</a>. In a fresh directory, install Node.js 22 or later and run <code>npm install --ignore-scripts pg@8.23.1</code>. Set <code>PG_URL</code> to an administrative connection on a disposable PostgreSQL server, then run <code>node concurrent-load-verify.mjs</code>. It needs database-creation permission and room for five test connections. It creates its own <code>rel3_a28_run*</code> database, writes <code>raw-results.json</code> beside the worksheet, closes its connections and drops that database. Supply your own connection when repeating elsewhere; the fallback addresses this local verification instance.</p>

